1
00:00:00,031 --> 00:00:15,136
[SPEAKER_00]: There's an interesting set of questions we received, which basically pertains to the way to prepare yourself to practice, how to practice, and then also how to look for the right environment for your practice.

2
00:00:17,097 --> 00:00:27,241
[SPEAKER_00]: And as we, yeah, as we covered most of those things in various talks, it would probably be, it would be a good idea to mention it all together.

3
00:00:39,042 --> 00:00:47,029
[SPEAKER_00]: As the question specifies, it says how to establish the right knowledge base for three things.

4
00:00:47,829 --> 00:00:57,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Leading suttas, develop a reflexive mindset, which is a mindset of mindfulness, and advice on entering a monastery.

5
00:00:57,750 --> 00:01:01,513
[SPEAKER_00]: Where you can obviously perfect these practices.

6
00:01:03,154 --> 00:01:06,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the first point is how to read the suttas correctly.

7
00:01:06,516 --> 00:01:11,760
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes down to recognizing that whichever way you start, it will not be perfect.

8
00:01:13,361 --> 00:01:20,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you're already starting with all the wrong views, wrong assumptions, and then you won't be able to

9
00:01:21,459 --> 00:01:24,801
[SPEAKER_00]: To begin in a manner that won't be revolving around those.

10
00:01:25,201 --> 00:01:38,066
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why it's important to maintain that as a context is because it will prevent you from drifting too far away from the original motivation that made you want to practice, which is suffering and freedom from it.

11
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:44,149
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you recognize that it's your experience as it is, at that very starting point.

12
00:01:44,709 --> 00:01:45,930
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the problem is.

13
00:01:46,798 --> 00:01:57,764
[SPEAKER_00]: So, eh... then, whatever you're learning through the suttas, through the instruction of... of... eh... of another, as... eh... in the form of, eh... the Emperor Buddha's teachers, or whatever.

14
00:01:58,184 --> 00:02:12,772
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, whatever you're learning about it, you will then, eh... maintain the correct context in recognizing that whatever you're learning about it, it should be pertaining to undoing that wrong starting point.

15
00:02:15,765 --> 00:02:28,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, what's going to happen is, if you don't question your starting point, you will take for granted all of your attitudes and notions about the practice, about the things that you should be practicing with.

16
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:32,648
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll be taking them for granted as if they are not already affected with ignorance.

17
00:02:33,128 --> 00:02:36,229
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you will be trying to build your practice on top of those.

18
00:02:36,829 --> 00:02:38,710
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why it won't work.

19
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:40,150
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

20
00:02:40,873 --> 00:02:52,898
[SPEAKER_00]: For example, it's like, say, you come across the suttas for the first time and you start reading the translation of the suttas, which is, you know, there's no problem there, but... and then he talks about the consciousness, talks about the matter.

21
00:02:53,558 --> 00:03:03,822
[SPEAKER_00]: See, the problem there is that when you think consciousness, when you think matter, when mind affected with ignorance thinks consciousness, that's not that which consciousness is.

22
00:03:04,662 --> 00:03:07,263
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, it might be to some extent, but

23
00:03:07,885 --> 00:03:11,026
[SPEAKER_00]: Already those notions are the notions affected with ignorance.

24
00:03:11,527 --> 00:03:27,394
[SPEAKER_00]: So, instead of you thinking that you can understand the suttas just by figuring out, oh, the relationship between consciousness or this or that, when you are thinking consciousness of form and trying to figure things out on account of it, those terms are already the terms that are affected with ignorance.

25
00:03:27,714 --> 00:03:29,475
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's that's a very important point here.

26
00:03:29,515 --> 00:03:36,158
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the practice, the direction of the practice, is the direction of undoing your

27
00:03:37,403 --> 00:03:53,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about finding the secret that's been hidden away from you.

28
00:03:53,451 --> 00:04:04,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really removing the assumptions and notions and significances that you yourself have been maintaining, that have been obscuring the nature of those things.

29
00:04:04,556 --> 00:04:05,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the nature of those things

30
00:04:06,348 --> 00:04:16,011
[SPEAKER_00]: It's properly understood, not at the end where you figure it all out, but in the very beginning, basically, when you undo all the wrong notions that have been covering it up.

31
00:04:16,571 --> 00:04:24,814
[SPEAKER_00]: If you keep that in mind, then the attitude of reading the suttas will be much... well, it won't be too too wrong to begin with.

32
00:04:24,854 --> 00:04:36,138
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're reading something about the consciousness, or reading about... or any sutta, whichever sutta you're reading, or whatever it might be about, you will then maintain a notion that if you truly understand the sutta, you will be at least a sotāpanna.

33
00:04:37,263 --> 00:04:44,449
[SPEAKER_00]: If you fully understand and develop the sutta and fully then develop the understanding, you'll be an arahant.

34
00:04:45,250 --> 00:04:51,615
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you have a notion that you understood the sutta, that is absolutely fine, because you probably did understand it to some extent.

35
00:04:52,196 --> 00:05:02,845
[SPEAKER_00]: But, unless then you know for sure that your understanding is on the level of freeing you from suffering and preventing you from being able to suffer even amidst things that used to cause you suffering...

36
00:05:02,885 --> 00:05:05,267
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless your understanding has reached that level, you shouldn't really

37
00:05:05,663 --> 00:05:06,683
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't be content with it.

38
00:05:06,983 --> 00:05:08,804
[SPEAKER_00]: You shouldn't really think that you understood it.

39
00:05:09,464 --> 00:05:13,645
[SPEAKER_00]: Because understanding, if done rightly, results in freedom from suffering automatically.

40
00:05:13,685 --> 00:05:15,746
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that is always your criteria to go back to.

41
00:05:16,846 --> 00:05:30,270
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the efforts towards contemplation, of understanding the instruction, clarifying, getting the meaning of the instruction for practice, those efforts should be then revolving around, eh... not, as I said, not figuring it out as something you're missing out, but more like

42
00:05:32,542 --> 00:05:35,663
[SPEAKER_00]: The things that you are using to figure these things out.

43
00:05:36,043 --> 00:05:49,025
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you're thinking about what is the nature of... what is like a nature of anatta, for example, when you say to yourself, okay, anatta means this, maybe you consult Pali, consult other translations, and then you get the idea of what anatta is.

44
00:05:49,105 --> 00:05:58,066
[SPEAKER_00]: But instead, using that idea to sort of arrive at some conclusion, you really want to be... keep questioning that idea as much as you can and go in the opposite direction.

45
00:05:58,126 --> 00:06:01,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Not to arrive at a conclusion, but undo any assumption you have about it.

46
00:06:03,295 --> 00:06:08,118
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can take any... you can literally take any Pali term, for example, and reflect on it like that.

47
00:06:08,378 --> 00:06:09,579
[SPEAKER_00]: Any relevant Pali term.

48
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:12,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you take, let's say, for example, rūpa.

49
00:06:13,822 --> 00:06:16,704
[SPEAKER_00]: In the suttas, it means form, it means... it's just like this.

50
00:06:16,784 --> 00:06:18,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Form is matter, sometimes means body.

51
00:06:18,746 --> 00:06:20,186
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you know what that means?

52
00:06:20,587 --> 00:06:29,713
[SPEAKER_00]: When you yourself, in your own experience, when you say rūpa, when you say matter, or whichever your native language is, whichever word you will be using,

53
00:06:30,315 --> 00:06:37,120
[SPEAKER_00]: Instead of assuming that you already know what it means, what you designated with that word, start questioning it.

54
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:42,504
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you say like, look, consciousness, consciousness, like unconscious, what does that mean to you?

55
00:06:43,185 --> 00:06:43,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you describe?

56
00:06:44,025 --> 00:06:44,546
[SPEAKER_00]: Can you see?

57
00:06:45,206 --> 00:06:51,111
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it that you refer to when you use a designation called consciousness?

58
00:06:52,312 --> 00:06:53,913
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you referring to in your experience?

59
00:06:53,933 --> 00:06:55,394
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you must be referring something.

60
00:06:56,055 --> 00:06:56,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise,

61
00:06:57,103 --> 00:06:58,764
[SPEAKER_00]: That board would be completely meaningless.

62
00:06:58,924 --> 00:07:00,744
[SPEAKER_00]: All the boards would be completely meaningless.

63
00:07:02,545 --> 00:07:04,725
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you should be reading the suttas as well.

64
00:07:05,305 --> 00:07:14,468
[SPEAKER_00]: You read the sentence, you read it too, you kind of understand it, but then see how deeply can you question that understanding while you're reading that sutta.

65
00:07:16,169 --> 00:07:23,871
[SPEAKER_00]: When the Buddha paints a scenario of like a, like a, oh, young man, subject to this, subject to that, he does this, and then he goes forth, and then you...

66
00:07:24,715 --> 00:07:29,437
[SPEAKER_00]: Take every individual sentence and then reflect on it in the direction that I just outlined.

67
00:07:29,477 --> 00:07:30,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Question it.

68
00:07:30,417 --> 00:07:37,639
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when it says in the suttas, oh, young man experienced dissatisfaction with life, am I experiencing dissatisfaction in life?

69
00:07:37,659 --> 00:07:44,602
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I take for granted that I already understood properly what dissatisfaction for life is?

70
00:07:44,642 --> 00:07:48,943
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, sure, I might have understood it to some extent, but

71
00:07:50,110 --> 00:07:51,410
[SPEAKER_00]: What is... what is it to say?

72
00:07:51,630 --> 00:07:56,652
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I able to say correctly that I understood it to the same extent like this young man in the suttas that the Buddha referred to?

73
00:07:57,292 --> 00:07:58,233
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you question it.

74
00:07:59,113 --> 00:08:01,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, don't take it for granted.

75
00:08:01,734 --> 00:08:18,759
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even... even for things as generic as dissatisfaction with life, you can exercise the same reflexive sort of contemplation and questioning, let alone then for the more specific terms like anatta and dukkha and all those... all those other... other key phrases from the suttas.

76
00:08:19,824 --> 00:08:23,047
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you... because that's how you come to understand them.

77
00:08:23,107 --> 00:08:32,915
[SPEAKER_00]: It's discerning them, removing the gratuitous assumptions, so you get to discern them on the level of your own experience, because these things are universal.

78
00:08:33,376 --> 00:08:44,465
[SPEAKER_00]: The suttas are basically just the most accurate descriptions of the universal aspect of every human experience.

79
00:08:44,925 --> 00:08:45,306
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

80
00:08:46,006 --> 00:08:46,687
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whether they were

81
00:08:47,243 --> 00:08:56,766
[SPEAKER_00]: They were spoken two and a half millennia ago, they were spoken five millennia ago, if they were spoken ten years ago, if they were spoken correctly, they would have always been pertaining to the same universal principles.

82
00:08:57,167 --> 00:09:05,349
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, it doesn't matter how much time passes between the utterance of the suttas and one understanding them, one will be understanding the same things.

83
00:09:05,529 --> 00:09:11,071
[SPEAKER_00]: And even the Buddha himself was... could not have done things differently.

84
00:09:11,111 --> 00:09:15,393
[SPEAKER_00]: He himself said that all that the Tathāgata can do is uncover

85
00:09:16,572 --> 00:09:23,438
[SPEAKER_00]: The universal truth, the universal path that has always been there, just been covered up, like a jungle, overgrown, the old ancient path.

86
00:09:26,220 --> 00:09:31,324
[SPEAKER_00]: So, keeping that in mind, then, you realize it's not about it arriving, it's something you're missing out.

87
00:09:31,805 --> 00:09:39,371
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really uncovering the place where you begin your practice from.

88
00:09:40,252 --> 00:09:45,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to practice now, the direction, sort of, collecting information from out there,

89
00:09:45,715 --> 00:09:46,336
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all fine.

90
00:09:46,356 --> 00:09:48,277
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to know what you're at least supposed to do.

91
00:09:48,858 --> 00:09:57,205
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, once you start doing it, it's basically about removing the... questioning the assumptions that you already have in regard to things that you are using for your practice.

92
00:09:58,907 --> 00:10:06,053
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when you say... when you read the sutta about meditation upon certain types of perceptions, you start asking yourself, okay, perception.

93
00:10:06,073 --> 00:10:07,775
[SPEAKER_00]: When I say perception, what am I referring to?

94
00:10:08,412 --> 00:10:10,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I referring to sight, sound, smell?

95
00:10:10,293 --> 00:10:12,394
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that... what are the characteristics?

96
00:10:12,414 --> 00:10:22,140
[SPEAKER_00]: What are the signs and tokens, as the Buddha would say, of those things that I will automatically take for granted if I start looking at the answers that will be provided for me?

97
00:10:22,660 --> 00:10:30,825
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not about the answers, it's about questioning the premises of your questions, even before you arrive at any answers.

98
00:10:32,106 --> 00:10:33,366
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with that kind of attitude,

99
00:10:34,841 --> 00:10:52,650
[SPEAKER_00]: The suttas will then start to serve as, basically, not as some sort of informational instruction, but as a direct, most accurate, most immediate description of the universal aspects of your experience here and now, of everyone else's experience here and now, be it in the past or in the future.

100
00:10:53,271 --> 00:10:55,092
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's how accurate suttas are.

101
00:10:55,632 --> 00:11:03,236
[SPEAKER_00]: But the reason why one is not able to understand them, to the point of developing the right view,

102
00:11:04,073 --> 00:11:06,935
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of your own wrong views and wrong assumptions.

103
00:11:06,995 --> 00:11:12,258
[SPEAKER_00]: So, wrong assumption about consciousness, about the five aggregates, about everything that you read in the suttas.

104
00:11:12,778 --> 00:11:16,301
[SPEAKER_00]: Most of the things already are infected with your own assumptions.

105
00:11:16,361 --> 00:11:17,541
[SPEAKER_00]: That is your starting point.

106
00:11:19,783 --> 00:11:22,965
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not to figure out the secret that's contained in the suttas.

107
00:11:23,005 --> 00:11:26,187
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically undoing your assumptions about the concepts that you read.

108
00:11:26,447 --> 00:11:31,330
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's how you then get to understand what those concepts refer to, what those concepts... what their nature is.

109
00:11:32,216 --> 00:11:40,513
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why in the suttas you get sometimes a person literally hears a sentence from the Buddha or two and immediately becomes a sotāpanna.

110
00:11:41,255 --> 00:11:43,760
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not because he was so lucky and the Buddha sort of

111
00:11:44,498 --> 00:11:47,360
[SPEAKER_00]: Tathāgata planted the truth in his head with using his psychic powers.

112
00:11:47,380 --> 00:11:50,241
[SPEAKER_00]: No, he said himself, Tathāgata cannot do the work for you.

113
00:11:50,261 --> 00:11:51,322
[SPEAKER_00]: He can point out the way.

114
00:11:51,882 --> 00:11:55,024
[SPEAKER_00]: If you make the effort, you'll arrive at the right view in Arahantship.

115
00:11:55,084 --> 00:11:56,024
[SPEAKER_00]: If not, you won't.

116
00:11:56,224 --> 00:11:57,645
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what Tathāgata says.

117
00:11:58,185 --> 00:12:08,251
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reason why those people would need such a little amount of instruction is because they did not carry all these wrong assumptions in regard to the terminology that they just heard Buddha utter.

118
00:12:11,467 --> 00:12:15,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that explains all those cases when, like people, upon hearing one sutta, that's it.

119
00:12:15,730 --> 00:12:16,571
[SPEAKER_00]: They became arahants.

120
00:12:17,251 --> 00:12:19,813
[SPEAKER_00]: It means they've already done the work of removing these assumptions.

121
00:12:20,674 --> 00:12:25,678
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, certainly, haven't done the wrong work of accumulating these assumptions.

122
00:12:25,738 --> 00:12:30,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So, how far your assumptions have been accumulated, you can't tell.

123
00:12:30,962 --> 00:12:33,984
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way to know that is in hindsight, once you have undone them.

124
00:12:35,025 --> 00:12:36,546
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how you should read the suttas.

125
00:12:37,582 --> 00:12:46,348
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the second point, then, is how to develop the... how to practice mindfulness properly, how to develop the Reflexive Mindset.

126
00:12:46,468 --> 00:13:02,638
[SPEAKER_00]: In brief, the fundamental attitude for properly practice mindfulness is being aware of the motivation behind your current actions, whatever those actions might be.

127
00:13:03,263 --> 00:13:07,526
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about attending to your body, observing sensations and whatnot.

128
00:13:08,207 --> 00:13:21,076
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really maintaining that background knowledge of what is currently happening to you, which always involves a degree of your action, to some extent.

129
00:13:22,137 --> 00:13:23,318
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the basic principle.

130
00:13:23,358 --> 00:13:26,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is simply asking yourself, what am I doing right now?

131
00:13:28,541 --> 00:13:29,482
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I doing right now?

132
00:13:31,627 --> 00:13:35,870
[SPEAKER_00]: You will already know what you're doing, without needing to stop doing what you're doing.

133
00:13:37,571 --> 00:13:42,034
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the mindfulness is on the level of discerning that knowledge, that's already there.

134
00:13:42,754 --> 00:13:46,777
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, if you do mindfulness correctly, it is effortless, as we spoke so many times.

135
00:13:48,718 --> 00:13:49,418
[SPEAKER_00]: And the...

136
00:13:50,079 --> 00:13:56,903
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the reflexive mindset, then, is the mindset that keeps watchful eye not on the immediacy of your experience, as in

137
00:13:57,478 --> 00:13:59,979
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm touching now my legs and I'm sitting and I'm leaning.

138
00:14:00,019 --> 00:14:02,961
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to be saying all those things because you're already doing them.

139
00:14:03,421 --> 00:14:10,685
[SPEAKER_00]: All you need to do is find the elevated ground from which those things will be seen without needing to directly stare at them.

140
00:14:11,406 --> 00:14:16,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And that elevated ground is the motivation behind your current action.

141
00:14:16,569 --> 00:14:24,673
[SPEAKER_00]: All that elevated ground can then be rooted in anything else that acts as a basis for your action.

142
00:14:25,913 --> 00:14:27,554
[SPEAKER_00]: where your motivations are rooted.

143
00:14:27,574 --> 00:14:30,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Body postures, current moods, feelings, and so on.

144
00:14:31,856 --> 00:14:41,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's important... the important thing is to think that... to realize that it's not about trying to catch a moment and... and... and... and be... be so attentive to... to one moment, so you don't miss anything out.

145
00:14:41,582 --> 00:14:43,223
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is actually completely the opposite.

146
00:14:44,273 --> 00:14:50,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Direction, which is direction of knowing what you're already doing, without needing to directly attend and keep telling to yourself what you're doing.

147
00:14:50,859 --> 00:14:52,160
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you already know what you're doing.

148
00:14:52,440 --> 00:14:53,641
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the... that's the key here.

149
00:14:54,102 --> 00:14:55,603
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not fabricating that knowledge.

150
00:14:55,623 --> 00:14:56,384
[SPEAKER_00]: You're discerning it.

151
00:14:56,404 --> 00:14:58,065
[SPEAKER_00]: It has already been there.

152
00:14:58,445 --> 00:15:07,213
[SPEAKER_00]: And without that kind of knowledge of what's already happening to you, to something, doesn't mean you fully understood it, but means there is already a degree of some recognition.

153
00:15:07,774 --> 00:15:09,395
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's basically what mindfulness is.

154
00:15:09,698 --> 00:15:11,199
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you can't have too much mindfulness.

155
00:15:11,219 --> 00:15:18,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You have too... you can't have too much of a recognition of what's already happening to you, what you're already doing, what you want to do.

156
00:15:18,484 --> 00:15:32,072
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's also why, if you practice mindfulness like that, it will... you will become... well, it will... it will become... it will become impossible to not see where things that you're about to do, where those things are rooted.

157
00:15:33,113 --> 00:15:37,836
[SPEAKER_00]: You will... you won't be able to mistakenly or carelessly engage with unwholesome,

158
00:15:38,574 --> 00:15:45,120
[SPEAKER_00]: You will only be able to do so volitionally, knowingly, because now you know.

159
00:15:45,200 --> 00:15:47,082
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why ignorance is the most fundamental.

160
00:15:47,242 --> 00:15:48,303
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignoring.

161
00:15:48,363 --> 00:15:50,345
[SPEAKER_00]: Choosing to ignore what you know.

162
00:15:50,685 --> 00:15:52,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Choosing to ignore what's already there.

163
00:15:53,007 --> 00:16:00,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Choosing to ignore the certain motivation behind your action that you know is actually rooted in desire or ill-will and so on.

164
00:16:01,372 --> 00:16:10,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will be able to maintain that kind of choice of ignoring, because you don't have developed, you haven't practiced mindfulness sufficiently enough, you haven't developed it.

165
00:16:11,498 --> 00:16:30,668
[SPEAKER_00]: But if now your standard is to never be completely ignorant of your current situation, current motivation, current actions, means you will never be able to fully, completely commit then to choosing to ignore it, so that you can follow your desire, your ill-will,

166
00:16:31,693 --> 00:16:34,615
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the key here.

167
00:16:35,556 --> 00:16:40,279
[SPEAKER_00]: All of those unwholesome things are always rooted in your intention to do them.

168
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:46,103
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't accidentally do things rooted in desire, aversion and delusion.

169
00:16:46,483 --> 00:16:47,724
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you are responsible for it.

170
00:16:47,744 --> 00:16:54,649
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why the Buddha would say things like you must then avoid everything unwholesome and then cultivate the wholesome.

171
00:16:54,989 --> 00:16:57,171
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the key.

172
00:16:57,191 --> 00:16:58,271
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in the knowledge.

173
00:16:58,291 --> 00:16:59,232
[SPEAKER_00]: It's rooted in recognition.

174
00:16:59,292 --> 00:17:00,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where ignorance comes first.

175
00:17:01,835 --> 00:17:04,256
[SPEAKER_00]: You can only ignore something that's already there.

176
00:17:04,276 --> 00:17:05,877
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't ignore things that don't exist.

177
00:17:06,997 --> 00:17:10,299
[SPEAKER_00]: So, a certain recognition exists, but you choose to ignore it.

178
00:17:11,019 --> 00:17:14,221
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why a person is fully responsible for avijjā.

179
00:17:14,661 --> 00:17:20,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Avijjā is not on the level of a metaphysical secret that needs to be figured out.

180
00:17:20,203 --> 00:17:24,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Avijjā is the most immediate attitude that you have towards your own experience as a whole.

181
00:17:24,505 --> 00:17:25,846
[SPEAKER_00]: Attitude of ignoring it.

182
00:17:26,546 --> 00:17:27,667
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

183
00:17:27,707 --> 00:17:30,028
[SPEAKER_00]: Attitude of taking for granted your recent desire,

184
00:17:30,503 --> 00:17:32,104
[SPEAKER_00]: There is an aversion and so on.

185
00:17:32,404 --> 00:17:39,666
[SPEAKER_00]: By doing actions, by performing actions rooted in attitude of ignoring your own experience, that's how you maintain the ignoring of your experience.

186
00:17:39,786 --> 00:17:52,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you need to practice sense restraint, physical restraint, develop virtue, as in stop maintaining the wrong attitude through those unwholesome actions, and then you can develop proper mindfulness of that attitude, because it is already there.

187
00:17:53,751 --> 00:17:56,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you're doing right now, the motivation to do that,

188
00:17:57,537 --> 00:18:01,461
[SPEAKER_00]: The intention to do that is simultaneously present with it, on that level of the knowledge.

189
00:18:02,101 --> 00:18:05,585
[SPEAKER_00]: I still have an intention to carry on sitting here and answering these questions.

190
00:18:07,066 --> 00:18:11,770
[SPEAKER_00]: Once that intention changes, as in once I decide not to do it anymore, I'll stop doing it.

191
00:18:12,651 --> 00:18:23,441
[SPEAKER_00]: And now I don't need to be thinking about how am I sitting and how my back is touching the chair and so on, because all of those things are already contained within the intention to do this action.

192
00:18:24,354 --> 00:18:27,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's pretty much the only place you need to keep your eye on.

193
00:18:27,717 --> 00:18:32,901
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to monitor and try to catch all of these little things that are happening around you and control them.

194
00:18:32,921 --> 00:18:33,041
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

195
00:18:33,241 --> 00:18:43,750
[SPEAKER_00]: You just need to keep an eye on the... like peripherally, just be aware with a corner of your eye on that point where the action is rooted, which is in the intention for that action.

196
00:18:44,310 --> 00:18:52,757
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if my intention changes and starts going into something unwholesome, I would see it even before you even start doing anything remotely unwholesome.

197
00:18:54,108 --> 00:18:59,872
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you see it as on the level of that possibility of unwholesome, means it's not on you.

198
00:18:59,892 --> 00:19:03,354
[SPEAKER_00]: So you just... it's enough to recognize it at that level and you won't be acting out of it.

199
00:19:03,755 --> 00:19:06,416
[SPEAKER_00]: If you choose to act out of it, it cannot be accidental.

200
00:19:07,637 --> 00:19:09,699
[SPEAKER_00]: You will do so intentionally.

201
00:19:10,559 --> 00:19:11,180
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the thing.

202
00:19:11,220 --> 00:19:15,963
[SPEAKER_00]: The more you develop mindfulness, the less able you will be to intentionally engage.

203
00:19:16,977 --> 00:19:18,157
[SPEAKER_00]: With unwholesome actions.

204
00:19:18,657 --> 00:19:38,462
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where literally the Buddha sometimes, to some people, this was so clear to some people, that the Buddha, all he needed to say was, avoid everything that you know to be unwholesome, and cultivate everything that you know, as in, you're aware of it with the corner of your eye, you're seeing whether the intention is wholesome or unwholesome, and cultivate everything that's wholesome.

205
00:19:38,662 --> 00:19:39,122
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's it.

206
00:19:40,442 --> 00:19:44,283
[SPEAKER_00]: People contemplated like that, practiced that kind of attitude, and became arahants.

207
00:19:45,081 --> 00:19:50,104
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you cannot have greed, aversion, delusion persist in your mind, unless it's being maintained.

208
00:19:50,964 --> 00:19:55,706
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless it's being perpetuated and fed with bodily action, verbal action and mental action.

209
00:19:56,487 --> 00:20:04,971
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you have uprooted the intentionally choosing those types of actions, these things will have to fade away out of your mind.

210
00:20:05,091 --> 00:20:06,332
[SPEAKER_00]: They cannot remain there.

211
00:20:06,392 --> 00:20:08,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you want it or not, it doesn't matter.

212
00:20:08,813 --> 00:20:14,176
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the simile where the Buddha said if... if a hen sits on her eggs,

213
00:20:15,260 --> 00:20:22,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if she doesn't wish for her chicken to pierce through the shell safely and come into this world, they will, because she's sitting on her eggs.

214
00:20:23,785 --> 00:20:30,869
[SPEAKER_00]: And if the other hand doesn't sit on her eggs, even if she wishes, oh, may my chicks pierce out safely, they won't, because she's not sitting on its eggs.

215
00:20:31,450 --> 00:20:37,333
[SPEAKER_00]: So, even if a monk wishes that his mind be purified from unwholesome, it won't be, unless he's doing the work.

216
00:20:38,331 --> 00:20:45,512
[SPEAKER_00]: If he's doing the work, even if he doesn't wish that his mind results in liberation and purified from a wholesome, he will, because he's doing the work.

217
00:20:47,013 --> 00:20:48,273
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the reflexive attitude.

218
00:20:48,293 --> 00:20:55,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Just keep a corner of your eye to whatever you're currently doing, wherever your current actions are rooted in.

219
00:20:57,215 --> 00:21:02,396
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether it's passively enduring things done to you, experiencing, witnessing, whether you're actively doing...

220
00:21:02,756 --> 00:21:07,037
[SPEAKER_00]: Fundamentally, there is a certain intentionality involved in all of that, and that's on the level of knowledge.

221
00:21:07,057 --> 00:21:07,717
[SPEAKER_00]: You already know

222
00:21:08,972 --> 00:21:12,273
[SPEAKER_00]: what your situation is, to put it in a most general term.

223
00:21:12,733 --> 00:21:19,835
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where body postures are kind of natural anchors, as in natural foundation for whatever's happening to you.

224
00:21:20,276 --> 00:21:24,337
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever's happening to me now, it's on the basis that this body is seated.

225
00:21:25,397 --> 00:21:29,959
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to be attending to seated body, you just need to know that you are already seated.

226
00:21:31,819 --> 00:21:33,060
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that knowledge endures.

227
00:21:33,080 --> 00:21:35,180
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't need to keep repeating, I'm seated, I'm seated.

228
00:21:35,760 --> 00:21:36,741
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I doing now?

229
00:21:36,781 --> 00:21:37,861
[SPEAKER_00]: What's my body posture now?

230
00:21:38,426 --> 00:21:38,926
[SPEAKER_00]: It's seated.

231
00:21:39,567 --> 00:21:39,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

232
00:21:40,187 --> 00:21:43,329
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now there's this knowledge that I have established by answering it.

233
00:21:44,150 --> 00:21:45,331
[SPEAKER_00]: And this knowledge is still here.

234
00:21:45,731 --> 00:21:47,612
[SPEAKER_00]: And we can talk about different things now.

235
00:21:47,692 --> 00:21:49,013
[SPEAKER_00]: We can address different topics.

236
00:21:49,394 --> 00:21:55,818
[SPEAKER_00]: But a corner of my eye is still kept on the on the basis of the background that this body remains seated.

237
00:21:56,799 --> 00:22:00,862
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever happens to it, it will happen on the basis of being anchored in the seated position.

238
00:22:01,082 --> 00:22:01,442
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

239
00:22:02,025 --> 00:22:06,127
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to actually... you don't want to keep repeating and telling yourself, I'm seated, I'm seated.

240
00:22:06,587 --> 00:22:11,689
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to bring your mind to it, sure, but discern it peripherally, and then let other things occur.

241
00:22:11,809 --> 00:22:15,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Let your mind go, without you losing the sight that you're still seated.

242
00:22:15,911 --> 00:22:24,695
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the key is to start discerning that relationship between things you're attending and the background those things are rooted in, inevitably, whether you want it or not.

243
00:22:25,215 --> 00:22:27,656
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm still seated.

244
00:22:27,676 --> 00:22:31,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, my situation of having a seated body, while we are talking about all these things,

245
00:22:31,955 --> 00:22:35,918
[SPEAKER_00]: It has remained unchanged for, like, what, half an hour now, twenty-something minutes?

246
00:22:36,698 --> 00:22:37,799
[SPEAKER_00]: It has remained unchanged.

247
00:22:38,439 --> 00:22:45,944
[SPEAKER_00]: And your awareness can then come and go from it, but all you need to do is keep the, like, 0.1%, literally, of the background.

248
00:22:45,984 --> 00:22:48,786
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just not completely drift away.

249
00:22:48,926 --> 00:22:53,729
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you do drift away, you can recognize yourself for as long as the body remains seated.

250
00:22:56,231 --> 00:22:58,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the anchor has remained unchanged.

251
00:22:58,092 --> 00:23:00,574
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, in other words, your forgetfulness

252
00:23:01,472 --> 00:23:06,996
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to start being included in your mindful situation that you have established with the knowledge of it.

253
00:23:08,037 --> 00:23:14,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Because lapsing of the mind, as in attention sort of going, going, going, and then losing sight of itself, is inevitable.

254
00:23:14,062 --> 00:23:15,203
[SPEAKER_00]: That's how the mind works.

255
00:23:16,103 --> 00:23:20,905
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's when you remember, whether you will remember on the wrong basis or on the right basis.

256
00:23:20,985 --> 00:23:25,887
[SPEAKER_00]: If you remember on the right basis, that's what I mean when I say you will start forgetting things mindfully.

257
00:23:25,987 --> 00:23:30,629
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, you can only forget the content of the experience, not the nature anymore.

258
00:23:30,989 --> 00:23:34,010
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, if you fully develop that, the mind will be impenetrable.

259
00:23:34,030 --> 00:23:40,193
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be absolutely no greed, aversion, delusion left in it.

260
00:23:40,273 --> 00:23:45,375
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the mindfulness developed to the extent necessary for the final knowledge, as the Buddha would say it in Satipaṭṭhāna.

261
00:23:45,735 --> 00:23:53,578
[SPEAKER_00]: That kind of knowledge will then be understood and discerned as more fundamental even than your act of forgetting this or forgetting that.

262
00:23:54,399 --> 00:23:58,380
[SPEAKER_00]: So, your forgetfulness, your act of forgetting, will be mindful.

263
00:23:58,761 --> 00:24:00,461
[SPEAKER_00]: You will do it mindfully.

264
00:24:00,501 --> 00:24:03,383
[SPEAKER_00]: And you do it mindfully not when you overly attend it and try to control it.

265
00:24:03,723 --> 00:24:07,985
[SPEAKER_00]: You do it mindfully where you become unable to misunderstand it.

266
00:24:08,605 --> 00:24:08,985
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

267
00:24:09,385 --> 00:24:12,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Unable to infect it with your wrong assumption.

268
00:24:14,327 --> 00:24:15,588
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much all to it.

269
00:24:17,060 --> 00:24:29,268
[SPEAKER_00]: Once your behavior has been restrained with the rules and virtue and precepts, you don't want to be over committing in trying to control the content of your thought or of your actions.

270
00:24:29,728 --> 00:24:33,891
[SPEAKER_00]: You actually want to be discerning the intention simultaneously present with those things.

271
00:24:35,452 --> 00:24:43,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm still seated and we're still talking about it and it's still the same mindfulness or the same knowledge of the same seated position and during all this time.

272
00:24:44,717 --> 00:24:46,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And now, if you have doubt, for example, but is it?

273
00:24:46,737 --> 00:24:47,137
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it not?

274
00:24:47,197 --> 00:24:47,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it not?

275
00:24:48,318 --> 00:24:59,640
[SPEAKER_00]: That doubt cannot actually do anything to the seated body, because for as long as the body portion remains seated, the anchor of the discernment remains unchanged.

276
00:25:00,120 --> 00:25:02,200
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it becomes impenetrable to doubt as well.

277
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:09,481
[SPEAKER_00]: Doubt will penetrate it, whereby you start doubting it, and then you stand up, and then you start doubting.

278
00:25:09,501 --> 00:25:10,021
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I doing?

279
00:25:10,061 --> 00:25:14,362
[SPEAKER_00]: But again, even that, it's you will intentionally choose to give in

280
00:25:16,015 --> 00:25:33,284
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you go beyond doubt when you stop feeding the craving towards the doubt, not when you keep finding... not when you find a perfect answer that will appease the doubt once and for all.

281
00:25:33,844 --> 00:25:41,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Doubt is an attitude in regard to the content, which means nature of that content is inherently impenetrable by that doubt.

282
00:25:42,173 --> 00:25:49,598
[SPEAKER_00]: But you are the one who chooses to ignore that and feed the doubt by trying to provide the answers that doubt is pressing you towards.

283
00:25:50,138 --> 00:25:53,441
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why doubt is a hindrance.

284
00:25:53,521 --> 00:25:57,203
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... if you stop feeding it, it will stop exercising power over you.

285
00:25:57,243 --> 00:25:59,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Eh... same as with any other hindrance, actually.

286
00:26:01,106 --> 00:26:05,189
[SPEAKER_00]: The question was the, eh... well, choosing the right environment, the right monastery and so on.

287
00:26:06,850 --> 00:26:06,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Eh...

288
00:26:07,603 --> 00:26:17,407
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you have to recognize the limitations of lay life, the constant bombardment of engagements and other people and company, how to avoid, how to maintain solitude and so on.

289
00:26:17,447 --> 00:26:32,133
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you choose to stay a layman or a laywoman, you have to take up responsibility for it, which means you have to take up responsibility for limiting the extent you can develop your practice.

290
00:26:32,633 --> 00:26:36,235
[SPEAKER_00]: You just can't do it in such an environment, only to some extent.

291
00:26:37,532 --> 00:26:44,498
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, back in the day, when lives were not so bombarded and sensuality was not so accessible, it was still hard.

292
00:26:44,778 --> 00:26:46,419
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you read it in the suttas.

293
00:26:47,060 --> 00:26:51,223
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people could get the right view and so on, but even back then it was hard.

294
00:26:51,243 --> 00:27:03,413
[SPEAKER_00]: Imagine today, when everything is, like, a finger-click away and constantly, I mean, like, massive, massive... people live in massive cities with loads of... with loads of constant crowd in a park, with just constant, constant company, company.

295
00:27:03,854 --> 00:27:06,735
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you're not necessarily perceiving it directly anymore, it's there.

296
00:27:06,815 --> 00:27:09,797
[SPEAKER_00]: It's part of the significance of the society that you live in.

297
00:27:09,817 --> 00:27:15,119
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have to recognize the limitations and then take responsibility for it.

298
00:27:15,139 --> 00:27:25,764
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can't really complain, if your practice is not really developing to the extent you wanted to, because you chose to stay in the environment that is such.

299
00:27:25,985 --> 00:27:31,067
[SPEAKER_00]: Having said that now, that doesn't mean that going get into monastery will be a solution in itself either.

300
00:27:31,407 --> 00:27:32,948
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, monastery is just

301
00:27:34,333 --> 00:27:37,394
[SPEAKER_00]: intensifying environment for the work you need to do internally.

302
00:27:37,594 --> 00:27:40,495
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you're not doing the work internally, it doesn't matter.

303
00:27:40,515 --> 00:27:43,475
[SPEAKER_00]: If you spend the rest of your life in the monastery, you won't go... you won't get anywhere.

304
00:27:44,155 --> 00:27:52,618
[SPEAKER_00]: So, sometimes people think that all... all they need to do is find a perfect monastery, then the practice will... everything will fall into place, and they'll just be salient.

305
00:27:53,138 --> 00:27:55,638
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... that's completely... completely not the case.

306
00:27:56,579 --> 00:28:02,520
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the suitability of a monastery should be measured to the extent of whether you are able to maintain the virtue,

307
00:28:02,960 --> 00:28:21,464
[SPEAKER_00]: As in whether the community is keeping the vinaya rules, to the extent of whether you're just constantly engaging with lay people, and constantly sitting in the organized groups, and crowds, and community meetings, and community things, and doing things together, or whether you actually spend most of the day left alone, as in in solitude.

308
00:28:21,764 --> 00:28:29,206
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't mean left alone without any instruction and so on, but means that whether most of your time is in the environment

309
00:28:29,693 --> 00:28:32,936
[SPEAKER_00]: is within the environment, they will they will provide a physical solitude.

310
00:28:33,977 --> 00:28:39,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, sure, there are things, occasions, you come together, but you want, sort of, to constitute minor part of the day.

311
00:28:39,842 --> 00:28:44,606
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if everybody is perfectly silent and mindful, the fact is you're surrounded physically by others.

312
00:28:44,646 --> 00:28:51,032
[SPEAKER_00]: There's perception of others, and because of that, the mind won't be taking, developing notions and signs of solitude.

313
00:28:51,332 --> 00:28:56,377
[SPEAKER_00]: They're necessary to discern the sign of the mind, that is necessary to discern the right view and sotāpatti.

314
00:28:56,557 --> 00:28:56,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

315
00:28:57,895 --> 00:29:02,339
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the criterias for then the right place are relatively general.

316
00:29:02,820 --> 00:29:14,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the good vinaya and solitude, everything else in between, you know, when there is a will, there is a way, things are not perfect, you could still make it work, as long as these fundamental principles are not compromised.

317
00:29:15,331 --> 00:29:22,958
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's important to really see that there is no such thing as a perfect place, because you have absolutely no criteria what perfection is.

318
00:29:22,978 --> 00:29:23,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You're only

319
00:29:23,961 --> 00:29:34,172
[SPEAKER_00]: The criteria of a putujana who starts practicing, or even somebody who's practicing longer, or something, but who's not a sotāpanna, the criteria is, this pleases me, and this displeases me.

320
00:29:34,352 --> 00:29:36,834
[SPEAKER_00]: As the suttas often say.

321
00:29:36,974 --> 00:29:44,142
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't really... you shouldn't really be trusting that if something is pleasing to you, that means that it's right.

322
00:29:44,722 --> 00:29:47,105
[SPEAKER_00]: Or that if something is displeasing to you, it means that it's wrong.

323
00:29:49,142 --> 00:29:55,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that you will be experiencing things in such manner, but instead of just taking for granted your attitude towards it, question it.

324
00:29:55,905 --> 00:29:58,085
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there's something displeasing to you, start questioning.

325
00:29:58,125 --> 00:29:58,545
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is this?

326
00:29:58,565 --> 00:29:59,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Did the Buddha say this is wrong?

327
00:30:00,126 --> 00:30:02,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, why am I finding this displeasing?

328
00:30:02,587 --> 00:30:08,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Are my ideals that are not matched by whatever, like, all these things, are my ideals right?

329
00:30:09,129 --> 00:30:10,790
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this behavior against the vinaya?

330
00:30:10,830 --> 00:30:12,611
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this environment against the practice?

331
00:30:13,551 --> 00:30:13,991
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe.

332
00:30:14,151 --> 00:30:15,852
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, then, okay, you got your answer.

333
00:30:15,892 --> 00:30:18,553
[SPEAKER_00]: If it isn't, then, like, so why is it still displeasing to you?

334
00:30:19,331 --> 00:30:26,033
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people, you know, minds, everyone's mind, to some extent, will be looking for faults in things as well.

335
00:30:26,053 --> 00:30:29,594
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you'll be looking for faults, which will be revolving around your conceit, or...

336
00:30:29,614 --> 00:30:31,595
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why that self-honesty has to be important.

337
00:30:31,855 --> 00:30:41,238
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... even if things are overall displeasing to you, unless the criterias of vinaya and solitude are compromised, then you really should try and work with it.

338
00:30:42,218 --> 00:30:49,060
[SPEAKER_00]: As opposed to just going from a monastery to a monastery, or a place to place, or refusing to commit to it, because you still haven't found the perfect situation.

339
00:30:50,761 --> 00:30:58,323
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll never will, because the perfect situation will be created from the inside, on a certain sufficient basis.

340
00:30:58,363 --> 00:31:00,084
[SPEAKER_00]: The perfect situation won't be found outside.

341
00:31:00,144 --> 00:31:05,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Otherwise, all you need to do is get to a monastery to become an arahant, and everybody would be doing that.

342
00:31:07,065 --> 00:31:15,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I think that kind of sums up, in general, the attitude towards the suttas, the attitude towards mindfulness, which is necessary for discernment, and then tailoring that

343
00:31:17,643 --> 00:31:20,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Those criteria for... towards a search for the right environment.

344
00:31:21,005 --> 00:31:42,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if you don't necessarily commit to the monastery, you can certainly simplify your life as a layman, by keeping the precepts, reduce the amount of time you eat per day, don't eat for beautification, don't eat for the pleasure of the taste, but literally measure, recognize the necessity for macronutrients, for health, for strength,

345
00:31:42,640 --> 00:31:46,461
[SPEAKER_00]: For endurance, and then meet the macronutrients in the most efficient way.

346
00:31:46,481 --> 00:31:49,442
[SPEAKER_00]: And micronutrients as well, for that matter, but let's not get into diet now.

347
00:31:49,882 --> 00:31:53,283
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, don't, don't just... You could practice lots of restraint.

348
00:31:53,763 --> 00:32:02,666
[SPEAKER_00]: You could practice... You could set up a relatively sufficient environment, unless you're only committed to life in terms of family and everything else, in which case it would be even harder.

349
00:32:03,646 --> 00:32:10,488
[SPEAKER_00]: But you could set up even your, your, your lay environment to a great deal by simplifying your life, and that can certainly help you then.

350
00:32:11,326 --> 00:32:18,013
[SPEAKER_00]: An offer is a kind of a transitional step, if you're still unsure whether you want to join the monastery and so on.

351
00:32:18,654 --> 00:32:27,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, being aware of your situation will then enable you to judge these things rightly and practice sense restraint accordingly.

